{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BmFp2i96xGTDhWaxSKqvJh","type":"tool_result","content":"# Draft Reference Gap Memo\n\n**Status:** ready — three targeted retrieval needs.\n\nExisting coverage is adequate for design-based estimation (Aronow, Ugander, HT variants), GATE designs, and regression-based GATE estimation (Chin, Han and Ugander). Quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and are not flagged. The gaps below are the places where positioning or background statements are thinly supported.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Exposure-mapping misspecification and unknown interference\n- Foundational interference/spillover and bias of naive estimators\n- Variance and confidence-interval estimation under network dependence\n\n## Targets\n\n1. **thesis-exposure-mapping-misspecification** (introduction, priority HIGH, positioning)\n   The central thesis is that fidelity of the fitted exposure mapping governs accuracy. The corner-extrapolation account in Results supports it. Only Aronow2013EstimatingAC is cited for \"exposure mapping\", and nothing covers misspecified mappings, unknown interference, or extrapolation to the all-treated/all-control corners (overlap/positivity). Reviewers would expect the paper to position itself against that work.\n   - Anchor: \"accuracy is governed by the fidelity of the fitted exposure mapping to the generating mechanism\"\n   - Search topics: misspecified exposure mapping bias under network interference; average treatment effects under unknown interference; positivity/overlap extrapolation for global effects.\n\n2. **intro-interference-dim-bias** (introduction, priority MEDIUM, background)\n   The first paragraph states that network A/B tests violate no-interference and that DiM is systematically biased for the GATE. These are uncited foundational claims; only HT estimators are cited there.\n   - Anchor: \"The difference-in-means (DiM) estimator, which is unbiased without interference, is systematically biased...\"\n   - Search topics: causal inference with interference/spillovers; bias of difference-in-means in network A/B tests.\n\n3. **reliability-interval-undercoverage** (results, reliability subsection, priority MEDIUM, comparator)\n   Coverage of 0.839 against a nominal 0.95 is a headline negative result. The draft explains that HC1 intervals ignore cluster dependence and use no resampling, but it cites no work on variance estimation or confidence intervals under network dependence.\n   - Anchor: \"ignores cluster dependence under the cluster design and uses no resampling\"\n   - Search topics: variance estimation under network dependence in interference experiments; cluster-robust or network HAC standard errors.\n\n## Not flagged\nThe related-work paragraphs on design-based estimation, designs, and regression adjustment are densely cited. The HT, graph-cluster-randomization, and Chin/Han comparators are covered. GNN/Facebook100 mentions appear only in limitations as untested extensions and need no added support.\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n# Paper Strategy (paper_standalone, ICLR 2026)\n\n**Status:** ready. **Provisional title:** How Accurate Is Regression-Based GATE Estimation Under Network Interference? Exposure-Mapping Fidelity Governs Error in an Eight-Case Grid.\n\n## Central question\nIn a single randomized experiment on a network, how accurately can a regression-based GATE estimator recover the truth, and which features of the interference mechanism, graph and assignment design decide when it works and when it fails?\n\n## Central thesis\nOn the eight-case synthetic grid (n=3000; SBM / Barabasi-Albert / Watts-Strogatz; additive, strong, saturating, degree-dependent and two-hop interference; Bernoulli and cluster assignment), regression-GATE error is small (about 3-5% relative RMSE per case) when the fitted exposure mapping matches the mechanism, and large when it does not (saturating 0.198, count x degree 0.151, two-hop 0.452). Graph family, interference strength and cluster vs Bernoulli assignment did not degrade accuracy within the tested range. Nominal 95% intervals undercover (0.839). The frozen BIC-gated parametric exposure regression lowers mean relative RMSE to 0.126 vs 0.180 for plain linear exposure regression, but leaves the two-hop case unresolved.\n\n## Why this framing\n- Final evaluation: completed, outcome `qualified`, 24 unseen seeds x 8 cases, 0 missing/failed episodes. No `downstream_study` block, so no downstream claims. Qualification supports reporting the measured results only, not generality or superiority.\n- Rejected: \"new estimator wins\" (only 3 scored variants, same case specs in search and final, no gate ablation, no per-case baselines); search-chronology (diary). Chosen: thesis-first characterization with the frozen estimator as the studied method. Not edit mode, so prior-paper status is \"rejected\" (nothing to preserve).\n\n## Method groups\n1. Frozen BIC-gated parametric exposure-regression estimator (proposed/studied).\n2. Plain linear exposure regression (baseline, 0.180).\n3. Difference-in-means (0.622) and neighbourhood Horvitz-Thompson (0.994) (baselines).\n4. Correctly specified oracle regression (supporting reference, 0.045).\n5. Search-plane estimator-class alternatives: flexible ridge ensemble (0.232), linear + residual correction (0.185), vs BIC-gated library (0.140) and linear (0.184) on that plane. Ablation-style, labelled search plane.\n6. Negative controls and replay (supporting): constant 9.0e-15, permuted outcome 0.0588, permuted graph RMSE 0.627, 0 vacuity violations, 0 replay diff.\n\n## Results focus\nAggregate method comparison; per-case profile with single-factor contrasts (explicitly case pairs, no per-case intervals); aggregate bias 0.074 vs RMSE 0.126; interval undercoverage (0.839, width 0.379); seed-level variability from the 24 per-seed scores (two outliers, seeds 9001 and 9015); search-plane estimator-class table, kept separate from final.\n\n## Figures (ICLR is single-column; text width about 5.5 in)\n- **Fig. 1 overview_schematic** (generate-concept-figure, full width, page 1-2): pipeline and eight-case factor grid, with the corner-extrapolation vs misspecification error sources.\n- **Fig. 2 main_accuracy** (matplotlib, full width, 2 panels): (a) five methods aggregate relative RMSE, log/broken axis; (b) per-case frozen-estimator error across 8 cases (budget relaxed to 8 categories, 1 series, because the grid has exactly eight cases).\n- **Fig. 3 reliability** (matplotlib, 0.85 width, 2 panels): per-seed scores with bootstrap interval; coverage vs 0.95 nominal.\n- Tables: case-factor table with final per-case error; search-plane estimator-class table.\n- PDF + 300 DPI PNG from one source script; LaTeX references the PDF; no shrinking of dense figures.\n\n## Body-page use (8-9 pages, no padding)\nFraming + Fig. 1 (~1), related work (~0.75), setup/estimator/metric (~1.5), results (~3-3.5), discussion and limitations (~1).\n\n## Supplement\nCase specifications; exact estimator and baseline definitions; per-seed table and bootstrap; negative controls; search-plane comparison; LLM Usage section (required by venue).\n\n## Must-disclose limitations\nSearch and final share case specifications and the library was designed after seeing search-plane per-case errors (no transfer-to-new-mechanism claim); one n, one p; case-pair contrasts, not factorial; no per-case baselines, bias or intervals; two-hop failure cause unknown (gate diagnostics unverified, so unknown); undercoverage cause not established; no public-graph or real-data experiments, no gate ablation (ablation_studies empty), no learned-graph methods.\n\n## Avoid\nHeadings: Experiment A/B, search trajectory, iteration history, progression, campaign summary, lessons learned. Phrases: combined_score, correctness rate, eligible, rubric, program_1, \"we first tried\", state-of-the-art claims. Use \"model variant\", \"configuration\", \"seeded run\".\n\n## Writer notes\nCite only relevant bib entries (Chin, Aronow-Samii, Ugander, Karrer, Han-Ugander, Chen et al., Eichhorn et al., Forastiere et al., etc.) after checking provenance; many bib entries are irrelevant noise. Starter-list papers missing from the bib must not be cited without provenance. Anonymize everything. Full machine-readable plan in `paper_strategy.json`.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bb1e3ac1-2d41-49c3-91d2-3ceeea2cc7ae","uuid":"9dc1366b-5f53-4af4-8f7e-682e94821934","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:58:47.464Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Draft Reference Gap Memo\n\n**Status:** ready — three targeted retrieval needs.\n\nExisting coverage is adequate for design-based estimation (Aronow, Ugander, HT